findings.claim:
The first entry's three patterns (.taad/fitness.yaml:1176-1178) have no (?i). PowerShell operator names ignore case, so `-Contains`, `-In` and `-EQ` fold case exactly as `-contains` does: in PowerShell `@('NtfF') -Contains 'Ntff'` is True.

Measured against all ten patterns of both entries:
- `$all | Where-Object { $WatchedTags -Contains $_[0] }` (Read-Mark's filter at PoolWatch.ps1:212 with its c dropped, capitalised) gets 0 hits;
- `if ($name -In $WatchedTags) { $x = 1 }` gets 0 hits;
- `if ($t -EQ 'NtfF') { $x = 1 }` gets 0 hits;
- the lower-case `-contains` form gets 1 hit.

The second entry has (?i), but its patterns need a word `tag` or `$_[0]` on the left, and `$WatchedTags` ends in 'Tags', not 'tag'. The corpus spells these operators in lower case only (T10, T11, T13, T18). So the class O-018 calls closed misses the capitalised spelling of its own N3 row (T11/S50).
